#use(std/code)
#use(std/xref)

#section{Cross-References}::

#code{#use(std/xref)} provides #code{#def} / #code{#ref} / #code{#glossary} /
#code{#toc} — the two-pass showcase. #code{#def} records at collect time;
#code{#ref}, #code{#glossary} and #code{#toc} resolve in the second pass, so
document order does not matter: a #code{#ref} may precede its #code{#def}

#code(lang: "plain")
	A deterministic #ref("vm") runs the program — referenced before it is defined.

	We define #def("vm"){virtual machine}{a sandboxed execution environment}.

	#glossary

Live:

A deterministic #ref("vm") runs the program — referenced before it is defined

We define #def("vm"){virtual machine}{a sandboxed execution environment}

#glossary

(In this how-to set all pages share one info table, so the glossary above
also lists the RFC 2119 keywords registered by that page — cross-file
resolution is the feature, not a bug.)

Spelling of #code{#def}: the term is the #strong{first} body, the definition
the second — #code|{#def("vm"){term}{definition}}| or
#code|{#def("vm"){term}::}| with the definition as the hanging block. A
term-only #code|{#def("vm"){term}}| is allowed (empty definition). The
#code|{#def(id){term}: definition}| spelling from the design sketch is
#strong{not} a form — a call cannot combine a brace body with a
#code{: rest-of-line} body

#code{#toc} renders the section tree the same way (delayed, order-independent):

#code(lang: "plain")
	#toc

It is not used live here because a #code{#toc} reflects the #strong{entire}
doc-set — it would embed this whole how-to's outline into the page

#section{Spelling a ##ref}
	Four spellings; the brace body is the #strong{id} exactly when no explicit id
	is given elsewhere, otherwise it is the custom link text:

	#code(lang: "plain")
		#ref("vm")                 host-computed / quoted id; text = the def's term
		#ref{vm}                   brace-body id — the terse everyday form
		#ref("vm"){custom text}     positional id + custom link text
		#ref(to: "vm"){custom}      keyword id + custom link text

	Ids match #strong{case-insensitively} — #code|{#ref("VM")}| resolves
	#code|{#def("vm")}| — but the #strong{definition's} casing is authoritative:
	the link text is the def's term, and the anchor keeps the def's own spelling
	(#code|{#def("VM")}| anchors at #code{def-VM})

#section{Aliases (##defalias)}
	#code|{#defalias(target alias …)}| registers extra ids that resolve to
	#code{target} — plurals and alternate spellings, one definition, one glossary
	entry. Variadic: every argument after the first is an alias. It emits nothing,
	and like #code{#ref} it is order-independent (the target may be defined later)

	#code(lang: "plain")
		#defalias("vm" "vms" "v-m")

	Arguments are #strong{space}-separated, not comma-separated: a #code{,} is
	Racket's #code{unquote} inside #code{(…)}

#section{Scopes (##xref-scope)}
	By default every #code{#def} registers at the document #strong{root}, so a
	definition in one file resolves a reference in another (cross-file resolution
	is the feature). #code{#xref-scope::} opens an inner scope: #code{#def}s inside
	it register #strong{there}, a #code{#ref} resolves by walking outward from
	where it sits — nearest definition wins — and the same id in an inner scope
	#strong{shadows} an outer one instead of being a duplicate error

	#code(lang: "plain")
		#def("x"){the outer x}

		#xref-scope(name: "api" ref-class: "api")
			#def("x"){the API x}
			Inside, #ref{x} is the API x.

		Outside, #ref{x} is the outer x again.

	A def inside a scope is #strong{invisible} outside it (an unresolved #code{#ref}
	is a dangling-reference error, as always). #code{name:} makes the scope
	addressable — #code|{#glossary(scope: "api")}| lists that scope's definitions
	instead of the root's. #code{ref-class:} is a styling hook (below)

	Duplicate ids are still an error #emph{within one scope}; across scopes they
	shadow. Aliases obey the same rule, so a scope may define its own aliases

#section{Styling refs}
	Every #code{#ref} link carries #code{class="xref-ref"}, plus the
	#code{ref-class:} token of the scope its #strong{definition} lives in and of the
	scope the #strong{reference} sits in — so a scoped ref renders as
	#code|{<a class="xref-ref api" …>}|. Styling is a #strong{default rule attached
	to that hook}, never baked into the content, so a scope can override it

	Each backend expresses the hook its own way:

	#list
		#item
			#strong{HTML} — the class rides on the #code{<a>}; style it with CSS.
			#code{yamd/backend/default.css} is a #emph{reference} stylesheet
			(#code{a.xref-ref} is italic, #code{a.xref-ref.api} shows a per-scope
			override)
		#item
			#strong{Typst} — refs render as
			#code|{#link(<anchor>)[#yamd-ref(class: "…")[text]]}|.
			#code{yamd/backend/default-preamble.typ} is a #emph{reference} preamble
			defining #code{yamd-ref} (default: #code{emph}); redefine it or branch on
			the class list to restyle per scope
		#item
			#strong{Markdown} — CommonMark cannot carry a class, so refs are
			#strong{italic} by default. Host code can override the whole rendering via
			the #code{current-markdown-styled-ref} parameter (and
			#code{current-typst-styled-ref} for Typst)

	The two reference assets are #strong{not} injected automatically — the backends
	emit document #emph{fragments}, so whoever assembles the final page or
	#code{.typ} includes them

#section{Linking to Another Document (##doc-ref)}
	#code{#doc-ref("path")} links to another #strong{document} of the set by its
	path from the build root (the CLI's #code{--root}, or the root document's
	directory), the #code{.yamd} extension optional. Delayed like #code{#ref}:
	the link text defaults to the target's title, a body overrides it, and a
	path with no matching document is a dangling reference — an error, never
	silent

	#code(lang: "plain")
		See the #doc-ref("stdlib/elements/tables"){tables guide}.

	No live demo here: the path is resolved against the #emph{build root}, so its
	exact spelling depends on how the set is built — this page's own path is
	#code{xref} when the links guides are built alone, #code{stdlib/links/xref}
	when nested under the language index. Unlike #code{#link} (a verbatim URL), the
	path is a document identity, so it survives the single/multi build split — an
	in-page anchor when the set is inlined into one page, a #code{page.html#…}
	cross-page link in multi-file — and file moves within the set. Pin
	#code{--root} to keep the spelling stable across sub-tree builds

Corner cases:

#list
	#item
		Ids are host code inside #code{(…)} — quote them: #code{#ref("vm")} or #code{#ref('vm)}; a bare #code{#ref(vm)}
		is a variable reference
	#item
		A dangling #code{#ref} is an error at resolve time, never silent empty text; a duplicate #code{#def} id errors at
		collect time — #emph{within one scope}. The same id in a nested #code{#xref-scope} shadows rather than collides
	#item: #code|{#ref(id){custom text}}| overrides the link text (default is the definition's term)
	#item
		The glossary lists every def with backlinks to each ref site. Backlinks follow #emph{resolution}, so a ref to a
		scoped def is counted under that def, not under a same-named one elsewhere
	#item
		In a doc-set the info table is shared, so a #code{#def} in one file resolves a #code{#ref} in another — anchors
		stay unique by construction (a scoped def's anchor is disambiguated if it would collide)
	#item
		#code{#doc-ref} targets whole documents, not sections; for a specific section, #code{#def}/#code{#ref} a term there.
		Its identity is the path from #code{--root} (a section's own anchor is not a public, stable id) — pin
		#code{--root} to keep ids stable across sub-tree builds
	#item
		A #code{#doc-ref} rendered without the doc-set builder (plain #code{yamd-file->html}) has no index and so is always
		dangling — like #code{#toctree}, it needs the CLI/builder
